d$$-dimensional subcube?\n\nIt is a folklore conjecture (see [3]) that $$\\gamma _d = 1/(d+1)$$ for every integer $$d \\geqslant 2$$, that is, that the upper bound above is best possible. In other words, to intersect the vertex-set of every $$d$$-dimensional subcube in $$\\lbrace 0,1\\rbrace ^n$$, one cannot asymptotically do better than to take every $$(d+1)^{\\text{th}}$$ layer. The following result disproves this conjecture, showing that in fact $$\\gamma _d$$ is exponentially small in $$d$$.\n\nTheorem 7.We have\n\n$$$$\\begin{equation*} \\gamma _d \\leqslant c^d \\end{equation*}$$$$\n\nfor all $$d \\in \\mathbb {N}$$, where $$c <1$$ is a constant. In fact, $$\\gamma _d < \\frac{1}{d+1}$$ for all $$d \\geqslant 8$$.\n\nA related question of Johnson and Talbot [10, Question 13] is as follows: is it true that for any $$\\delta >0$$ and integer $$d \\geqslant 2$$, there exists $$n_0 = n(d,\\delta) \\in \\mathbb {N}$$ such that for all $$n \\geqslant n_0$$, every subset of $$\\lbrace 0,1\\rbrace ^n$$ of density at least $$\\delta$$ must contain at least $${d \\atopwithdelims ()\\lfloor d/2\\rfloor }$$ points of some $$d$$-dimensional subcube? It is easy to see that the answer to this question is ‘yes’ for $$d=2$$ and $$d=3$$. Indeed, any subset of $$\\lbrace 0,1\\rbrace ^n$$ of density greater than $$1/n$$ must contain two points at Hamming distance 2, by averaging over Hamming spheres of radius 1, and two such points are clearly contained in a common two-dimensional subcube. Moreover, any subset of $$\\lbrace 0,1\\rbrace ^n$$ of density greater than $$2/n$$ must contain three points of the form $$x\\Delta \\lbrace i\\rbrace,x\\Delta \\lbrace j\\rbrace,x\\Delta \\lbrace k\\rbrace$$ for some $$x \\in \\lbrace 0,1\\rbrace ^n$$ and distinct $$i,j,k \\in [n]$$, again by averaging over Hamming spheres of radius 1, and three such points are clearly contained in a common three-dimensional subcube.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------------